========================================================================
  THE CROSS-ORBIT CORRECTION
========================================================================

  CROSS-ORBIT MATRIX (gcd of cycle lengths):

  gcd | 3   5   7   9   11  13  
  --------------------------------------------------
  3   | 3   1   1   3   1   1   
  5   | 1   5   1   1   1   1   
  7   | 1   1   7   1   1   1   
  9   | 3   1   1   9   1   1   
  11  | 1   1   1   1   11  1   
  13  | 1   1   1   1   1   13  

  The cross-orbit correction for (3,3) is 2^{gcd(3,3)} = 2^3 = 8.
  For (3,5) it's 2^{gcd(3,5)} = 2^1 = 2.
  For (3,7) it's 2^{gcd(3,7)} = 2^1 = 2.
  For (5,5) it's 2^{gcd(5,5)} = 2^5 = 32.
  For (3,3,3) it's 2^{3+3+3} = 2^9 = 512.

  When both cycles are the SAME prime p: cross = 2^p (LARGE).
  When coprime: cross = 2^1 = 2 (SMALL).
  This means: same-prime multi-cycles interact STRONGLY,
  while different-prime cycles interact WEAKLY.

========================================================================
  TOURNAMENT ZETA FUNCTION: FORMAL DEFINITION
========================================================================


  FORMAL TOURNAMENT ZETA FUNCTION:

  Z_T(s, n) = Π_{k=3,5,7,...} (1 - R_k(n) × k^{-s+1})^{-1}

  This is an Euler product over ODD cycle lengths (= "tournament primes").

  At s=1: each factor ≈ 1/(1 - R_k) ≈ 1 + R_k (for small R_k).
  Product ≈ 1 + Σ R_k = 1 + single-cycle correction.

  The ACTUAL T(n) has extra cross-orbit factors.

  Key insight: The "primes" of tournament theory are
  the ODD INTEGERS k=3,5,7,9,11,...
  NOT the actual primes 3,5,7,11,13,...

  But! The cross-orbit matrix gcd(k,j) = 1 when k,j coprime.
  So coprime odd cycles interact WEAKLY (factor 2^1).
  Same-prime powers interact STRONGLY (factor 2^p).

  This means the tournament primes ARE the actual odd primes,
  in the sense that the cross-orbit interaction is minimal
  between different prime families.

  CROSS-ORBIT CORRECTION FACTOR C(n) = T(n) / Π(1+f_k):

  n= 3: C(n) = 1.0000000000
  n= 4: C(n) = 1.0000000000
  n= 5: C(n) = 0.9795918367
  n= 6: C(n) = 1.0280469148
  n= 7: C(n) = 1.0151489752
  n= 8: C(n) = 1.0042640743
  n= 9: C(n) = 1.0009450386
  n=10: C(n) = 1.0001483694
  n=11: C(n) = 1.0000191354
  n=12: C(n) = 1.0000022827
  n=13: C(n) = 1.0000002563
  n=14: C(n) = 1.0000000276
  n=15: C(n) = 1.0000000029

